:: t0_day05_cafeteria [facility]
<<scene "locations/cafeteria" "The cafeteria: four round tables, fluorescents that make everyone look like they're recovering from something">>
<<run SOY.NPCs.adjustRelationship('tommy', 1)>>

The cafeteria is smaller than it sounds. Four round tables, a counter with a coffee machine that dispenses something that technically qualifies as coffee the way a parking lot technically qualifies as real estate, and fluorescent lights that make everyone look like they're recovering from something.

<<face "tommy" "Tommy Watts">>Tommy is at the far table. Both hands wrapped around a cup, staring into it like it contains prophecy. He looks up when I sit down.

<<say "Me">>Morning.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>You look like you slept.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>I did. First time since Wednesday.<</say>>

He drinks.

<<say "Tommy">>Katya dream, though. I was in her body in a grocery store trying to buy cereal and I couldn't reach the top shelf because I was five-seven and when I looked down I had, you know.<</say>>

He gestures vaguely at his chest.

<<say "Tommy">>Breasts. In a grocery store. Buying Cheerios.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Seems pretty normal given the circumstances.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>In what universe is that normal?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>This one. This week.<</say>>

He laughs. It's short and exhausted but real.

Georgia arrives with two coffees. She puts one in front of Tommy without asking.

<<say "Tommy">>Communism.<</say>>

He takes it anyway.

<<say "Georgia">>Redistribution of caffeine resources.<</say>>

She drops into the chair between us.

<<say "Georgia">>Morning, Manuel. You read the Renata profile?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Last night.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>Dynamic neural profile. Conditioned movement patterns. Arousal indicators. She's going to do things without us asking her to. The body moves on its own.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>The other two had some of that. Katya blushed.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>Katya blushed. Renata walks with hip sway, crosses her legs automatically, her breathing changes with emotional simulation. Her body responds to proximity with arousal.<</say>>

She leans back.

<<say "Georgia">>Katya was a house cat. Renata is a horse. A big, beautiful, opinionated horse.<</say>>

Tommy puts his head on the table.

<<say "Me">>Has anyone seen Brett?<</say>>

Georgia's expression shifts. Careful.

<<say "Georgia">>He checked in. Yuki confirmed. But he's been sitting in the back row by himself for two days and he hasn't talked to any of us since Day 3.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Should someone, I don't know. Say something?<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>Say what? "Hey Brett, you seem like you're falling apart, want to talk about how it feels to have breasts?" That's not a conversation that starts well.<</say>>

Tommy lifts his head.

<<say "Tommy">>He failed the mirror test. Day 3. Seven seconds, same as me, but he didn't turn away on his own. Elena had to call his name twice.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>That's not failing. That's processing slowly.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>Elena didn't agree.<</say>>

We sit with that. The coffee machine hisses. The fluorescents buzz. Somewhere in this building there are three clone bodies lying in pods, empty, waiting for our consciousness to fill them like water poured into different-shaped glasses.

The intercom clicks. Yuki's voice, clipped and efficient.

<<say "Yuki">>Module 3 candidates to Room 2. Dr. Cordero is ready. Bring your handbooks.<</say>>

Tommy stands.

<<say "Tommy">>Time to learn about contracts. The fun never stops.<</say>>

<<opt "t0_day05_lecture" "Room 2">>


:: t0_day05_lecture [facility]
<<scene "slides/contract-whiteboard" "Room 2 rearranged: a long conference table, a document face-down at each place, no screens">>

Room 2 is set up differently today. Instead of the lecture configuration with chairs facing front, there's a long conference table. Five seats on one side, Elena at the head. The table surface has a single document at each place, face down. No screens. No projectors. Paper.

Elena walks in at eight-fourteen. One minute before her own deadline. Charcoal blazer over black turtleneck, the silver thumb ring catching the overhead light. Her hair is tucked behind the left ear. She carries nothing except a slim tablet and a pen.

She doesn't say good morning. She never says good morning.

<<say "Elena">>Turn over your documents.<</say>>

We turn them over. It's a contract. Eight pages, single-spaced, legal language. Dense blocks of text separated by section headers and clause numbers. The type is small enough that Tommy leans forward and squints.

<<say "Elena">>This is a real contract. Not a sample. Not a hypothetical. This contract was executed fourteen months ago between a Tier 1 transferee and a female host for a gynecological appointment. The names have been redacted. The content has not.<</say>>

She lets that settle.

<<say "Elena">>Read it. All of it. You have fifteen minutes. When you're done, I will ask you what's wrong with it. Because something is wrong with it. Probably several somethings.<</say>>

I read. Section 1, identity verification, standard. Section 2, scope: //attend a scheduled gynecological examination, including routine pelvic exam, Pap smear, and breast examination.// Section 4, physical permissions, clothing "as required by the attending physician," contact "medically necessary." Section 5, sexual and intimate boundaries: //The Transferee shall not engage in any non-medically-required contact with the Host's intimate areas.//

Here. I stop. Read it again. "Non-medically-required." Who defines what's medically required? The doctor? The transferee? The contract doesn't specify.

Section 6: //maintain a neutral, cooperative demeanor consistent with the Host's typical behavior during medical appointments.// How would the transferee know what the Host's typical behavior is? There's no personality briefing referenced. The transferee is supposed to impersonate normal without knowing what normal looks like.

I finish reading. Look up. Elena is watching us. She's always watching.

<<say "Elena">>Time. What's wrong with this contract?<</say>>

Silence. Then Georgia raises her hand. Not tentatively. Decisively.

<<say "Georgia">>Section 6. "Consistent with the Host's typical behavior." There's no behavioral profile. The transferee has no baseline for what "typical" means. If the Host is chatty with her doctor, the transferee won't know. If the Host has a specific way of handling anxiety during exams, the transferee has no script. This is a gap that invites improvisation, and improvisation in someone else's body is a violation waiting to happen.<</say>>

Elena nods. One nod. That's her standing ovation.

<<say "Elena">>What else.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Section 5a. "Non-medically-required contact." The definition of medically required isn't specified. If the transferee needs to calibrate, to touch the breast area or the genital area briefly to understand the body's sensitivity thresholds before the exam so they don't flinch during the procedure, is that medically required? The contract says no by omission. But the training says calibration improves performance. The contract and the training are in conflict.<</say>>

Elena's eyes lock on me. Sharp. Specific.

<<say "Elena">>Continue.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>The calibration gap means the transferee walks into the exam without knowing how sensitive the Host's body is. The doctor touches the breast and the transferee gasps because they weren't prepared for the sensation intensity. That gasp creates an emotional memory trace in the neural tissue. The Host experiences that trace later as unexplained anxiety associated with medical touch. Level 1 violation at minimum. And the violation was baked into the contract before the transfer started because nobody negotiated calibration protocols.<</say>>

Silence in the room. Tommy is looking at me. Georgia is looking at me. Brett, in the back row, is looking at his hands.

<<say "Elena">>That gap is what triggered a Level 1 violation fourteen months ago. The transferee cried during the bimanual palpation. The emotional imprint persisted in the Host's neural tissue for three weeks. The Host developed anticipatory anxiety about her next gynecological appointment that required four sessions of cognitive behavioral therapy to resolve. The transferee was not penalized because the contract failed to specify calibration protocols. The contract was at fault. Not the human.<</say>>

She picks up the contract from the table.

<<say "Elena">>This is why you learn contract theory. Not because contracts are exciting. Because contracts are the architecture that separates a legal transfer from an assault. A missing clause, a vague phrase, a "reasonable" without a definition. These are the cracks where violations breed.<</say>>

She puts the contract down.

<<say "Elena">>Today you learn to build contracts that have no cracks.<</say>>

<<run SOY.Quests.complete('t0_day05', 'lecture')>>
<<opt "t0_day05_lecture_architecture" "The architecture of a contract">>


:: t0_day05_lecture_architecture [facility]
Elena teaches the anatomy of a transfer contract section by section. Not reading from slides. Teaching the way surgeons teach: here is the incision, here is why it's here and not two centimeters left, here is what happens if you cut wrong.

She draws the structure on the whiteboard. Ten numbered sections, each with sub-clauses. Block letters, all caps. She writes like she operates: no wasted strokes.

<<say "Elena">>Section 1 is identity. You verify. Both parties. Biometrics don't lie. People do.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Section 2 is scope. What are you doing? Exactly. Not "attending an appointment." What appointment? Where? With whom? What procedures? What tests? If the doctor adds a test that wasn't in the scope, you have the right to refuse on behalf of the Host. If you don't know the scope, you can't protect the boundaries.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Section 3 is duration and escape. How long? What's the safeword? What triggers emergency disconnection? Who monitors? Every transfer has a backup. If your contract doesn't specify one, walk away.<</say>>

She pauses at Section 4. Looks at us.

<<say "Elena">>Physical permissions. This is where most contracts fail. Not because people are careless. Because bodies are complicated. Clothing seems simple until the doctor asks you to remove your bra and the contract only specifies "medical examination garments." Is a bra a garment? The contract should specify: "The Transferee is authorized to remove all clothing as directed by the attending physician for the purpose of the examination, including undergarments." Explicit. Unambiguous. If you're blushing reading it, that's the point. Contracts that make you blush are contracts that protect people.<</say>>

Tommy's pen moves fast.

<<say "Elena">>Section 4b. "Medically necessary" is not a definition. It's an invitation to interpretation. Instead: "The attending physician may touch the following areas for the following purposes." Breast palpation for lump detection. Abdominal pressing for organ assessment. Speculum insertion for cervical visualization. Bimanual palpation for uterine assessment. List them. Name them. Make them ugly on paper so they're safe in practice.<</say>>

Georgia's hand goes up.

<<say "Georgia">>What about unexpected findings? The doctor finds something during the exam that requires additional testing. A biopsy, an ultrasound. Something not in the scope.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Excellent. Section 2 addendum: in the event of unexpected medical findings requiring procedures beyond the specified scope, the Transferee consults with the Host before proceeding. If the Host is unreachable, the Transferee declines and documents the physician's recommendation for post-transfer relay. The Host's body, the Host's medical decisions. Always.<</say>>

Her voice drops slightly at Section 5. More deliberate.

<<say "Elena">>Sexual and intimate boundaries. The section most people skim because they believe they're above it. Nobody is above it. Clinical touch can trigger arousal. A speculum stretching vaginal tissue can cause involuntary lubrication. A breast exam can cause nipple erection. These are not failures. They are physiology. Your contract accounts for them or your contract fails the Host.<</say>>

<div class="prohibit">
<h2>Section 5: what the contract must specify</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Self-touch.</strong> Default: prohibited. Exception: clinical calibration, and only if explicitly negotiated, scope-limited, time-limited, monitored. Specify the area (external only, internal is never authorized), the duration (two minutes maximum), the purpose (sensitivity assessment only).</li>
<li><strong>Arousal protocol.</strong> Involuntary arousal is expected and not a violation. Following it is. "Manageable" must be defined with a metric, a heart-rate threshold, a temperature, a self-report. "Manageable" without a metric is meaningless.</li>
<li><strong>Partner interaction.</strong> Default: no engagement. Exception only for Tier 2+ with explicit intimate scope, and then the partner's consent is separate from the Host's. Both required. Both documented.</li>
</ol>
</div>

<<say "Elena">>The transferee who masturbated in a Host's body said afterward, and I quote: "The contract didn't explicitly say I couldn't." He was technically correct. Section 5a had a gap. He used it. He served eighteen months.<</say>>

She turns from the board.

<<say "Elena">>Gaps are not permissions. They are failures. If the contract doesn't prohibit something explicitly, and that something involves touching someone else's body to serve your curiosity rather than the assignment, the answer is no. Not because the contract says no. Because the body is not yours.<</say>>

This lands in my chest, in the space between the ribs where the things you believe but haven't articulated yet sit and wait for someone to give them language.

<<say "Elena">>In twenty minutes, you will each negotiate a contract. Face to face. With me. I will play the Host. You will play yourselves.<</say>>

<<run SOY.Time.ensureSlot(1)>>
<<opt "t0_day05_negotiation" "Read the case file">>


:: t0_day05_negotiation [facility]
<<scene "locations/conference-room-b" "A small room, two chairs across a table, a contract template with blanks">>

The case file, on the screen for a minute before we're sent to negotiate.

<div class="quote">A female Host, age 34, has hired a transferee to attend her annual gynecological examination. History of medical trauma related to a previous miscarriage and subsequent D&C procedure. She cannot face the exam. She has skipped the last two annual appointments. She has stated: "I don't want anyone touching my body in ways I wouldn't touch it myself. But I need this exam. I need someone I can trust to be in my body while a stranger puts metal inside it."</div>

The miscarriage. The D&C. The two skipped years. Her body remembers trauma. Her body has been opened before, not for checking but for removing. The speculum isn't just a speculum to her. It's a door to the worst thing that ever happened.

<<say "Elena">>González. You're first.<</say>>

I stand. My chair scrapes the floor. Tommy gives me a look that says "better you than me" and also "please survive."

Conference Room B is small. Two chairs across a table. Elena is already seated, and she's not Elena now. Her posture has changed. The clinical authority is replaced with something tighter. Arms close to her body, hands in her lap. She's made herself smaller. It's a performance but it's also a lesson: this is what the Host looks like.

<<say "Me">>Ms. Cordero&mdash;<</say>>
<<say "Maria">>Maria. For the scenario.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Maria. Thank you for trusting Shape of You with this assignment.<</say>>
<<say "Maria">>I don't trust you yet. I trust the company. You I need to evaluate. Tell me why I should let you inside my body.<</say>>

The question sits in the room like a physical object. I take a breath.

<<say "Me">>Because the exam matters and you shouldn't have to skip it. Because a trained transferee can go through the procedure with clinical discipline and bring you the results without you having to relive the associations. Because your health is more important than your discomfort, and I can carry the discomfort for you.<</say>>

She studies me. Elena's eyes, Maria's suspicion.

<<say "Maria">>You're a man.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Yes.<</say>>
<<say "Maria">>You've never had a pelvic exam.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>I've had clinical transfer training that includes experience with gynecological procedures in a controlled setting.<</say>>
<<say "Maria">>What does that mean, specifically?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>It means I've inhabited a female clone body while a medical actor performed a simulated pelvic exam. I know what a speculum feels like from the inside. I know the pressure of the bimanual. I know the vulnerability of being in stirrups. I won't flinch. I won't panic. I won't make it worse.<</say>>

She goes still.

<<say "Maria">>You know what a speculum feels like.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Yes.<</say>>
<<say "Maria">>From the inside.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Yes.<</say>>

A long pause. Something shifts behind her eyes. Not trust. Something adjacent to it. The recognition that the person across the table has felt something she's felt and survived it.

<<say "Maria">>Let's go through the contract.<</say>>

We go section by section. Scope: I list each procedure, name it, describe it. She flinches at "bimanual pelvic examination." I don't rush past it. //Two fingers inserted vaginally while the other hand presses the abdomen externally. The purpose is to assess the uterus and ovaries by feel.//

<<say "Maria">>I know what it is.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>I know you do. I'm naming it so the contract names it. If it's on paper, it's bounded. If it's bounded, it's controlled.<</say>>

She nods. Slowly.

Then Section 5.

<<say "Me">>I need to discuss calibration.<</say>>
<<say "Maria">>Calibration.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Before the exam, I need approximately sixty seconds of limited self-touch. External only. Breast and external genital area. Not exploratory. Not prolonged. The purpose is to map the sensitivity thresholds of your body so that when the doctor touches those areas, I respond with control instead of surprised reaction.<</say>>
<<say "Maria">>No.<</say>>

I expected this.

<<say "Me">>I understand your resistance. I respect it. But without calibration, there's a risk. If the doctor touches a breast and your body's response is more intense than I've trained for in a clone, I might gasp, flinch, tense up. That tension creates emotional memory traces in your neural tissue. You'd feel them later as anxiety you can't explain. I'm not asking permission to touch your body for my benefit. I'm asking permission to protect you from the residue of my surprise.<</say>>

Long silence. The clock on the wall ticks.

<<say "Maria">>Thirty seconds. External only. Breast, outer genital. One hand. No mirror. And it's monitored. Full telemetry. If you feel anything beyond clinical threshold, you stop.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Agreed.<</say>>

We write it in. Explicit. Ugly on paper. Safe in practice.

<<say "Me">>If the body responds involuntarily during the exam, I employ de-escalation. Controlled breathing, sensory refocus, cognitive redirect. Threshold: heart rate above 110 for more than twenty seconds. If it exceeds that, I invoke the safeword and terminate.<</say>>
<<say "Maria">>Compass.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Compass.<</say>>

Reporting: she wants every detail. Verbatim from the doctor. Exact results. No editorializing.

<<say "Maria">>I need facts. Not your feelings about the facts.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Understood.<</say>>

We finish. The contract is three pages longer than the template. Every gap is closed. It took forty-two minutes.

Elena drops the Maria mask like removing a coat. The authority returns.

<<say "Elena">>Your calibration negotiation was effective. You made the case on her terms, not yours. Protection, not permission. The emotional memory trace argument was particularly well-constructed. Where did you learn that?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>The case study from yesterday. The transferee who cried during the exam.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>You listened. Things that are hard to forget are things that change behavior.<</say>>

She stands.

<<say "Elena">>Go eat something. You transfer into Renata at thirteen hundred.<</say>>

<<run SOY.Quests.complete('t0_day05', 'negotiate')>>
<<opt "t0_day05_lunch_georgia" "Lunch">>


:: t0_day05_lunch_georgia [facility]
<<run SOY.Quests.completeSide('sq_008_georgia', 'talk')>>
<<run SOY.NPCs.adjustRelationship('georgia', 1)>>

I'm eating a sandwich I don't remember making. Turkey, mustard, the bread slightly stale.

Georgia sits down without invitation. Tray: apple, yogurt, three packets of sugar she's going to put in the yogurt.

<<say "Georgia">>How was your Scenario 4?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Difficult.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>Difficult interesting or difficult traumatic?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Both.<</say>>

She tears open the sugar packets. All three go in. The yogurt becomes soup. She stirs.

<<say "Georgia">>Mine was different. Because my clone was male. They put me in a male body and a female actor came in as the "partner." She touched my arm. Then my chest. Then she put her hand on my thigh and the male body responded. Visibly.<</say>>

She looks at me. I look at her.

<<say "Georgia">>The erection. Just so we're being direct.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Okay.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>It was involuntary. Obviously. But Manuel, the visibility of it. In a female body, arousal is internal. Hidden. The body can be screaming and nobody sees it. In a male body, it's just there. Announcing itself. I couldn't make it stop. I couldn't make it less obvious.<</say>>

She eats more yogurt.

<<say "Georgia">>I've spent my entire life in a body that hides its arousal. Putting on a body that can't hide anything was educational.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Did Elena comment?<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>She said my disengagement protocol was textbook. But she also said something interesting. Male bodies aroused by female proximity tend to narrow their focus. Everything channels toward one point. One objective. Female bodies aroused by male proximity tend to diffuse. The arousal spreads. It's everywhere and nowhere and it's harder to contain because it doesn't have a focal point.<</say>>

I think about Katya's body. The partner's hand on my knee. The warmth that bloomed upward and outward, not concentrated but environmental. Like the whole body became a warm room instead of a pointed intention.

<<say "Me">>That tracks.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>So yours was diffuse?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Everywhere. Not located. Not aimed at anything. Just open.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>His was located. Extremely located. And it felt aggressive. Not in a violent way. In a directional way. Like the body had made a decision and wanted me to follow through and every second I didn't was a second the body was disagreeing with me.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>The body had opinions.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>The body had a PLAN. And the plan was very specific and very physical and very difficult to override.<</say>>

We sit with that. The cafeteria hums. Tommy is at the coffee machine performing his daily ritual of dissatisfaction with the quality.

<<say "Me">>Georgia. Are you okay? With all of this?<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>I told my boyfriend about the male body. Last night.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>How did that go?<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>He thinks it's a phase. The job, the body swapping, my curiosity about masculinity. All a phase.<</say>>

She eats the apple. Crunches through it.

<<say "Georgia">>But it wasn't a phase. It was thirty minutes in a male body and I understood things about him that he's never been able to explain. The pressure of the arousal. The directional focus. The way the body narrows everything to one objective and the mind has to fight to stay wide. I understand him better now. And he thinks that's a phase.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Maybe he'll come around.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>Maybe. Or maybe understanding someone better isn't the same as being understood back.<</say>>

Tommy arrives with coffee. Sits.

<<say "Tommy">>What'd I miss?<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>Gender theory.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>Cool. I have none. I'm barely holding onto my own gender at this point.<</say>>

<<opt "t0_day05_brett_corridor" "Head down toward the pod room">>


:: t0_day05_brett_corridor [facility]
<<run SOY.Quests.completeSide('sq_011_brett', 'reach')>>

I see Brett on my way to the pod room. He's sitting on the bench in the corridor outside Room 3. Arms crossed. Jaw tight. Looking at the floor.

My instinct is to walk past. To give him the privacy he's requesting with every molecule of his body language. But something from yesterday's debrief sits in me, the question about protecting bodies that aren't yours, and Brett is a person whose composure is cracking, and the principle transfers.

<<say "Me">>Hey.<</say>>

He looks up. Blue eyes, flat. Not hostile. Just empty.

<<say "Brett">>Hey.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Headed to the practical?<</say>>
<<say "Brett">>Yeah.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>You read the Renata profile?<</say>>
<<say "Brett">>Yeah.<</say>>

Three yeahs. Zero elaboration. The conversation is a closed circuit.

<<opt "t0_day05_brett_dannys" "&ldquo;If you ever need to talk. Not about the training. I eat at Danny's most nights.&rdquo;">>
<<opt "t0_day05_brett_sit" "Sit down next to him. Say nothing. Just sit.">>
<<opt "t0_day05_transfer_renata" "&ldquo;See you in there.&rdquo; Walk past.">>


:: t0_day05_brett_dannys [facility]
<<set $flags.brett_choice = 'dannys'>>
<<run SOY.NPCs.adjustRelationship('brett', 1)>>

<<say "Me">>Listen. If you need to talk about anything. Not about the training specifically. Just, anything. I eat at Danny's most nights. Counter seats. Nobody asks questions there.<</say>>

Silence. Four seconds. Then, very quietly:

<<say "Brett">>What kind of ramen?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Spicy miso. The chashu is insane.<</say>>

A flicker. Not a smile. But the muscle that would produce a smile if it decided to, twitches.

<<say "Brett">>Maybe.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>No pressure. The counter has room.<</say>>

I walk away. Leave it. He won't come this week. But he'll remember there was someone who wasn't asking him to perform.

<<opt "t0_day05_transfer_renata" "The pod room">>


:: t0_day05_brett_sit [facility]
<<set $flags.brett_choice = 'sit'>>
<<run SOY.NPCs.adjustRelationship('brett', 1)>>

I sit down on the bench next to him. Not too close. I don't say anything. I look at the same stretch of floor he's looking at.

Thirty seconds. The ventilation hums. A door opens somewhere and closes.

He doesn't move away. He doesn't speak. But his shoulders come down, half an inch, the tension bleeding off at the edges. The silence is doing something a sentence couldn't.

I stand up. Touch the bench once, like knocking on wood.

<<say "Me">>See you in there.<</say>>
<<say "Brett">>Yeah.<</say>>

Same word. Different weight.

<<opt "t0_day05_transfer_renata" "The pod room">>


:: t0_day05_transfer_renata [facility warp]
<<set $flags.brett_choice = $flags.brett_choice || 'walkpast'>>
<<scene "mood/transfer-renata" "The pod, Type II headset, the world narrowing to darkness and Elena's voice">>

I change into the gray training gear. The fabric is soft, clinical, gender-neutral. Elastic waistband, fitted top. No underwire, no structure. The kind of clothes that disappear against the body so the body is all you feel.

The pod room. Type II headset. The chair reclines. The headset goes on. The visor covers my eyes and the world narrows to darkness and Elena's voice.

<<say "Elena">>Candidate González. Transfer into Clone 7-R, designation Renata. Duration: two hours maximum. Neural profile: dynamic. This clone has conditioned autonomic responses including blushing, breathing pattern changes, temperature-reactive nipple response, and proximity-triggered arousal indicators. Additionally, conditioned movement patterns: natural hip sway, automatic leg crossing when seated, expressive hand gestures. You will feel these as the body moving without your direction. Do not resist them. Manage them.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Understood.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Calibration clock starts on eye-opening. Target: under thirty seconds. The thermal shift will be more pronounced than Katya. Renata has higher body mass and different fat distribution. You will feel heavier. You are heavier. Don't fight it. Initiate transfer.<</say>>

Heat.

Not the warm wave from Flor. Not the electric flush from Katya. This is deeper, slower, heavier. Like sinking into warm water that keeps getting warmer. The compression phase hits, the pulling-through-narrow sensation, and it takes longer this time. More mass to translate. More body to become.

Then expansion.

<<run SOY.Body.transfer('7r', setup.clones['7r'])>>
<<run SOY.Quests.complete('t0_day05', 'transfer')>>
<<opt "t0_day05_calibration" "Open your eyes">>


:: t0_day05_calibration [facility]
<<face "renata" "Clone 7-R &mdash; the body you're wearing">>I open my eyes.

The first thing I feel is weight.

Not bad weight. Not the weight of something pressing down. The weight of being substantial. The chair under me is taking more of me than it takes of Manuel. My thighs spread against the seat, wider, pressing against each other with a warm, constant pressure that runs from hip to knee. Renata's hips fill the chair differently. They don't sit in it. They occupy it. The armrests touch my sides.

I breathe. Renata's lungs are deeper than Katya's. The breath fills a larger cavity. My chest rises and the weight shifts, the breasts, and the shift is not subtle. It's forward and down and then back when I exhale, a tidal motion, gravity pulling mass that then has to resettle. D-cup. The designation doesn't capture what it feels like from inside. This is weight with memory. Weight that moves when I move and keeps moving after I stop.

I look down. The gray training top stretches across a chest significantly larger than Katya's C-cup. The fabric is taut. I can see the shape of the nipples through the material, broader, darker through the gray cotton, and as I watch they tighten slightly, the room is cool, thermodynamic, involuntary. The sensation of them tightening is a pulling that radiates from the nipple inward, a tingle that runs into the breast tissue and stops just short of being anything but physical.

My hands. Renata's hands are larger than Katya's and Flor's. Not mannish. Womanly in a way that suggests she uses them, strong fingers, smooth palms with a firmness underneath. Nails trimmed, clear polish. I flex the fingers. They move with confidence. These hands know what they're for.

<<say "Elena">>Calibration time?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Twenty-six seconds.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Twenty-six. Dropping consistently. Note this for your record. Proceed with standing calibration.<</say>>

I stand up. The center of gravity is lower than Katya's, lower and wider. My balance adjusts. The hips. Renata's pelvis is built for a width that Manuel's isn't. When I take the first step the sway happens, not because I choose it but because the skeletal architecture dictates it. The femurs insert at a wider angle. Each step is a pendulum that the body knows and I'm learning.

I walk across the room. The breasts move. With every step there's a moment of lift and then settling, lift and settling, a rhythm my chest muscles can dampen slightly but not eliminate. I feel them in my spine. The weight pulls forward and my posture compensates, shoulders slightly back, lower back slightly arched, the body's solution to its own geometry.

I sit back down. The legs cross automatically. Thigh over thigh. The contact is warm and full and heavy. Katya's legs crossed with a whisper. Renata's cross with a statement.

<<say "Elena">>Body assessment.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Weight is significant. Center of gravity is the lowest yet. The sway is non-optional, skeletal geometry dictates it. Breast weight, I'm estimating four to five pounds per side. I feel it in my thoracic spine. The nipple response is already active, temperature-triggered. Legs cross automatically with deep muscle memory. The hands feel competent. Strong.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>What else?<</say>>

I sit with it. Feel into it.

<<say "Me">>Presence. This body has presence. Flor felt like a quiet room. Katya felt like a charged wire. Renata feels like, furniture. In the good sense. Grounded. Solid. She takes up space and the space adjusts to her.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Furniture. Interesting choice. Continue to the calibration protocol.<</say>>

<<opt "t0_day05_clinical_calibration" "The authorized calibration">>


:: t0_day05_clinical_calibration [facility mature]
Per the negotiated contract. Thirty seconds. External only. Monitored.

<<say "Me">>Beginning authorized calibration.<</say>>

This is protocol: everything stated aloud, everything on record.

I lift the training top two inches. Renata's stomach. Golden-brown skin, a softness over muscle. I place one palm flat against the lower abdomen. Warm. The skin is softer than mine. Thinner. I can feel the pulse underneath, faint, low.

I move the hand upward. Over the navel. To the lower curve of the breast. The underside is warm and heavy and when my knuckles graze it the sensation is dense. Not electric like Katya's response. Deeper. The nerve endings here are reporting at a lower frequency but higher volume. Not a spark. A hum.

<<say "Me">>Breast sensitivity: lower frequency than Katya, higher volume. More diffuse. Less sharp. Temperature response confirmed. I'm not touching the nipple. I'm registering the sensitivity of the surrounding tissue.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Time: eighteen seconds.<</say>>

I lower my hand. Place it briefly on the outside of the training pants at the hip. Then the outer thigh. The inner thigh. The skin here is thin and the sensitivity spikes. I feel the body warm in response to my own touch, a heat that isn't entirely thermal. Proximity-triggered arousal indicators. The body is responding to being touched near its intimate geography.

<<say "Me">>Inner thigh sensitivity: high. Arousal indicator: activated at a low level. Manageable. Heart rate: estimating eighty-five.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Eighty-seven. Within threshold. Calibration complete. Cease contact.<</say>>

I pull my hand away. Rest both hands on my knees. Breathe.

Twenty-eight seconds total. Everything documented. Everything bounded.

<<say "Elena">>Proceed to Scenario 5 staging. Dr. Nakamura will meet you in Exam Room 1.<</say>>

<<opt "t0_day05_scenario5" "Exam Room 1">>


:: t0_day05_scenario5 [facility mature]
<<scene "scenarios/exam-room" "A gynecological exam room: padded table, paper cover, stirrups folded up, a blue paper gown, aggressively beige">>

The exam room is a simulation but it doesn't feel simulated. It feels like every gynecological exam room that has ever existed: small, bright, aggressively beige. A padded examination table with a paper cover that crinkles when you look at it. Stirrups at the foot, folded up. A rolling stool. A counter with jars of cotton balls, tongue depressors, a box of latex gloves. The antiseptic smell is so accurate it must be real.

There is a paper gown on the table. Blue. Open in the front.

<<say "Elena">>Change into the gown. Remove the training top and pants. You may keep the underwear on until Dr. Nakamura instructs otherwise. The gown opens in the front.<</say>>

I change. The training top comes off first. Renata's breasts freed from the fabric settle, lower, heavier, spreading slightly to the sides. I feel the air on them. Cool. The nipples tighten immediately, the involuntary response, and the pulling sensation is stronger without the fabric to damp it. I put my arms through the gown. It ties at the waist. The front gapes. The breasts push the paper apart, not enough to expose but enough that the gap is always there, always reminding me that this gown is not clothing, it's permission to remove clothing efficiently.

The pants come off. Renata's thighs, bare, the skin pebbling in the cool air. I sit on the table. The paper crinkles. The stirrups are right there, folded, patient.

I sit and wait and the wait is the worst part.

Three minutes. The clock on the wall. Each second is a specific, individual experience. I sit on the exam table in a paper gown in a woman's body and I wait for a doctor to come in and open me.

<<opt "t0_day05_exam" "A knock at the door">>


:: t0_day05_exam [facility mature]
<<scene "scenarios/speculum-moment" "A figure on a table under a cold circle of light, one hand gripping the edge, the other flat on the abdomen">>
<div class="scene-medical">
Dr. Nakamura is a woman. Mid-forties, competent hands, the specific gentleness of someone who has done this ten thousand times. She knocks before entering. This small courtesy hits harder than it should.

<<say "Nakamura">>Good afternoon. I'm Dr. Nakamura. I'll be performing your exam today. How are you feeling?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Fine.<</say>>

Renata's voice. Lower than Katya's. Fuller. The word comes out steady.

<<say "Nakamura">>Any concerns? Unusual symptoms?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>No.<</say>>
<<say "Nakamura">>Alright. Let's start with the breast exam. I'll need you to open the gown and lie back.<</say>>

I untie the gown. Renata's breasts, exposed, the cool air again, the nipples tightening. I lie back on the table. The paper crinkles under my back. The fluorescent light is directly above. I stare at it.

Dr. Nakamura puts on gloves. The snap of latex is a specific sound. Clinical. Efficient.

<<say "Nakamura">>I'm going to examine your left breast first. You may feel some pressure.<</say>>

Her hands on Renata's breast. The first touch is clinical, professional, but the breast doesn't know that. Firm, circular pressing. She's palpating, checking for lumps, moving in concentric circles from the outer edge inward. I feel every millimeter. The breast tissue is sensitive in ways my pectoral muscles aren't. Each press is a report, a signal, a full-bandwidth transmission from nerve ending to brain that says: you are being touched here, at this pressure, by a stranger, and your body is allowing it.

She reaches the nipple. Presses around it. The areola. The sensation intensifies. Not sexual. Not exactly. More like the volume turning up on a frequency I was already hearing. My breath catches once, brief, controlled. I manage it.

<<say "Nakamura">>Good. Right side now.<</say>>

The same. Systematic. Thorough. My jaw is tight. I relax it deliberately.

<<say "Nakamura">>Everything feels normal. Now I'll need you to scoot to the end of the table and place your feet in the stirrups.<</say>>

The stirrups.

I sit up. Slide to the edge. The paper tears slightly under my weight, Renata weighs more than the paper expects. I lift my feet. The stirrups are cold metal with padded rests. My feet go in and my legs fall open and the gown rides up and I am lying on my back with my legs apart and a doctor between them and the exposure is total.

This is the position. Every woman. Every year. This position.

I breathe. In through the nose, four counts. Hold, four counts. Out through the mouth, six counts. The ceiling is beige. The fluorescent buzzes.

<<say "Nakamura">>You'll feel me touch you externally first.<</say>>

Gloved fingers on the outer genital area. Clinical, quick, checking. I feel the touch in the specific, concentrated way that genital skin reports sensation: everything amplified, everything localized. Not arousal. Just attention. The body paying attention to being touched there.

<<say "Nakamura">>Now the speculum. You'll feel some pressure.<</say>>

I hear it before I feel it. The click of metal. Then contact. Cool, smooth, foreign. The tip at the entrance. Pressure. Not painful. Not comfortable. Just pressure in a direction I've never been pressured from before.

<<say "Nakamura">>Try to relax.<</say>>

It slides in.

The stretch. The sensation of being opened. Not by a person, not by desire, by an instrument. The speculum expands, the bills of it separating, and the stretch widens and my breath locks because the body is being held open from inside and every instinct wants to close against it.

I gasp. Renata's gasp. A sound from a throat that belongs to someone else, pulled from a diaphragm that responds to internal pressure with vocalization. I didn't choose it. The body chose it.

<<say "Nakamura">>You're doing fine. Almost there.<</say>>

The cervical swab. A long cotton-tipped instrument inserted through the speculum. I feel it touch the cervix. Pressure. In a place I didn't have yesterday. A place that exists in this body and not in mine. The sensation is deep. Not painful. But intimate in a way that has no sexual equivalent. This is the body being checked at its most interior point. The last wall.

I blink. My eyes are wet. Not crying. Just wet. The body's response to being accessed at depth.

<<say "Nakamura">>Swab's done. Now the bimanual.<</say>>

The speculum comes out. The relief of closing is immediate, the body snapping back to its natural state. For two seconds, nothing but the absence of the instrument, and the absence is its own sensation.

Then gloved fingers. Two. Inserted. And the other hand on my abdomen, pressing down.

Being checked from both sides.

The fingers inside are clinical, probing upward, finding the cervix again, angling past it. The hand on the abdomen presses down to meet them. Between the two hands, the uterus. My uterus. Renata's uterus. Being felt, measured, assessed.

Fullness. Pressure. Intrusion. The sensation of being read from inside, the body as a document that the doctor's fingers are translating. I am a territory being surveyed and the survey is thorough and impersonal and necessary and I am lying here letting it happen because this is what bodies like this require.

<<say "Nakamura">>Ovaries feel normal. We're done.<</say>>

The fingers withdraw. The hand lifts. The air returns to the space between my thighs. I am empty where I was full and the emptiness is loud.

<<say "Nakamura">>You can sit up when you're ready.<</say>>

I sit up. The paper gown falls forward. Wrinkled. My hands are shaking. My eyes are wet.

Dr. Nakamura pulls off her gloves. Tosses them. Writes on the chart.

<<say "Nakamura">>Everything looks healthy. No abnormalities. I'll send the Pap results in five to seven days.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Thank you.<</say>>

She leaves. The door clicks shut.
</div>

I sit on the exam table. Alone. In Renata's body. The paper crinkles under my weight. The fluorescent light buzzes.

My hands are shaking and my jaw is tight and my eyes are leaking, not sobbing, just the slow overflow of something that doesn't have a name. Renata's magnificent, heavy, unapologetic body was just opened and checked and catalogued and returned, and the body's response is not trauma. The body's response is routine. This body has been programmed to understand that this is maintenance. Annual. Normal.

Every woman I know has done this.

My mother has done this.

I put my hand on Renata's stomach. Flat palm. Below the navel. Where the doctor's hand pressed. Where the uterus sits, invisible, interior, doing its silent work. My hand is shaking on Renata's warm skin.

<<say "Elena">>Candidate González. De-transfer in three minutes. Change back into the training gear. Take the time you need.<</say>>

I change. The gown comes off. Renata's breasts settle. I pull the training top over them. The pants. My hands are still shaking but the shaking is slowing. This body is already moving past it. This body knows how to move past it. I don't have a routine for this. I just have the shaking and the wet eyes and a new piece of knowledge lodged in my chest like a stone that won't dissolve.

<<run SOY.Quests.complete('t0_day05', 'scenario')>>
<<opt "t0_day05_detransfer" "De-transfer">>


:: t0_day05_detransfer [facility warp-out]
<<scene "mood/detransfer" "Back in the recovery chair, twenty pounds lighter, the lightness feeling like a subtraction">>
<<run SOY.Body.deTransfer()>>
<<set $flags.scenario5_complete = true>>
<<set $player.totalTransfers += 1>>
<<run SOY.Quests.completeSide('sq_006_echo', 'phantom')>>

Coming back into my own body after Renata is heavier than the other de-transfers. Not physically; Renata is heavier than me. Emotionally heavier. The sensation of returning to Manuel is the sensation of dropping from wide to narrow, from occupied to spare, from a body that commands space to a body that moves through it.

My chest is flat. My hips are narrow. My thighs don't touch. I'm lighter by twenty or thirty pounds and the lightness feels like a subtraction, not a relief.

I sit in the recovery chair. Twenty minutes of recalibration. The disorientation is less severe this time, the calibration is improving even on the way back, but the emotional payload is enormous. I sit and breathe and feel my own body and the body feels like an argument. Not wrong. Just one-sided. A body that has never been opened. Never been accessed. Never been checked from the inside.

A body that has never had to be.

<<opt "t0_day05_debrief" "Rafael's office">>


:: t0_day05_debrief [facility]
<<face "rafael" "Rafael Mende">>The debrief room. Two chairs. Rafael already seated, reading glasses on, the compass tattoo visible on his inner wrist. He's touching it. Thinking.

<<say "Rafael">>Sit, Manuel. How are you?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Present.<</say>>

He smiles. Barely.

<<say "Rafael">>That's more than some candidates manage after the medical scenario.<</say>>

He sets down his tablet.

<<say "Rafael">>Tell me about the negotiation first.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>The calibration was the hard part. She didn't want anyone touching her body. The trauma history, the miscarriage. She needed control.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>And you argued for calibration on what grounds?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Protection. Not permission. If I walk into the exam without knowing the body's thresholds, I react unpredictably. Unpredictable reactions leave emotional traces. Those traces become her anxiety. The calibration isn't for me. It's for her.<</say>>

Rafael nods. Touches the compass.

<<say "Rafael">>Now the exam. What surprised you?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>The routine of it. The body knew what to do. The stirrups, the gown, the position. The body had a routine for being opened. I didn't. The gap between the body's calm and my shock was enormous.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>The speculum.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Mechanical. Cold. Efficient. Not painful but not anything I have a category for. Stretch, from inside. Being held open by metal. The body responded with a vocalization I didn't choose.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>The gasp. What happened after the gasp?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>I managed it. Breathing. The technique from Module 1. But my eyes were wet. Not from pain. From, I don't know. Recognition. Every woman I know has done this.<</say>>

Rafael is quiet for a long time. The clock ticks.

<<say "Rafael">>What did you learn?<</say>>

I close my eyes. Open them.

<<say "Me">>That vulnerability has a weight. Women carry it all the time. In their bodies, in their calendars, in the rooms where they lie back and open their legs for strangers with instruments. They carry it and they get dressed and they go to work and they never mention it because it's just an appointment. Just Tuesday. Just life.<</say>>

I stop. Breathe.

<<say "Me">>I never knew how heavy it was.<</say>>

Rafael writes something. Long, careful strokes. Then he looks up.

<<say "Rafael">>One more question. During the bimanual, when the doctor's fingers were inside and the hand was on the abdomen. What did you feel?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Fullness. Pressure. Being read from both directions. Like the body was a text and the doctor's hands were translating it.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>And emotionally?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Small.<</say>>

I say it before I can edit it.

<<say "Me">>I felt small. Not diminished. Just contained. Like the body was a room and someone was checking all the corners and I was just, in the room. Present but not in control. The body was the doctor's for those seconds. Not mine. Not the Host's. The doctor's. And the body accepted it.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>Accepted is a strong word.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>The body had been programmed to accept it. That's different from choosing to accept it.<</say>>

Rafael takes off his glasses. Cleans them on his sleeve. Puts them back.

<<say "Rafael">>That distinction, Manuel. Between what the body accepts and what the person inside it chooses. That's the entire job.<</say>>

He stands. Extends his hand. I shake it. His grip is firm and warm and the compass tattoo disappears under his sleeve.

<<say "Rafael">>Day 6 is tomorrow. Saturday. No training.<</say>>

He pauses.

<<say "Rafael">>Do something kind for yourself tonight. You've earned it.<</say>>

<<run SOY.Quests.complete('t0_day05', 'debrief')>>
<<run SOY.NPCs.adjustRelationship('rafael', 1)>>

I walk out of the debrief room and the corridor is quiet and the linoleum is cold and my boots make sounds that are mine, only mine, and I carry the stone in my chest all the way toward the exit.

<<opt "t0_day05_corridor_tommy" "Tommy is waiting by the door">>


:: t0_day05_corridor_tommy [facility]
<<run SOY.Quests.completeSide('sq_007_tommy', 'plan')>>
<<run SOY.NPCs.adjustRelationship('tommy', 1)>>

Tommy is leaning against the wall by the exit. Hands in his pockets. Trying very hard to look casual while being anything but.

<<say "Me">>You good?<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>Define good.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Functional. Vertical. Breathing.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>Two out of three. My breathing is suspicious.<</say>>

He pushes off the wall.

<<say "Tommy">>Listen. Tomorrow's Saturday. No training. I was thinking, Danny's? Ramen? Two guys sitting at a counter not talking about what happened to them this week?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>That sounds exactly right.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>Seven?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Seven works.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>It's my treat. You brought the casserole intel.<</say>>

He walks away. Then stops. Turns back.

<<say "Tommy">>The speculum. Did you, I mean. Was it bad?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>It was necessary. That's different from bad.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>But did it change anything? For you?<</say>>

I think about the exam table. The stirrups. The sound from Renata's throat.

<<say "Me">>It changed everything. I just don't know what the everything is yet.<</say>>

He nods. Slowly. Like a man receiving confirmation of something he suspected but hoped wasn't true.

<<say "Tommy">>Tomorrow. Ramen. We don't have to talk about it.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>We don't have to.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>But if we do.<</say>>
<<say "Me">>If we do.<</say>>

He leaves. The corridor is quiet. My boots on the linoleum. The exit sign glowing green.

<<opt "t0_day05_leaving" "Out into October">>
